n: All-in (-2) immediately pre-flop\n\n**Hand Strength:**\nJ♠ 6♠ is a weak-to-mediocre hand:\n- It's suited, which gives flush potential\n- There's a 5-card gap between J and 6, limiting straight possibilities\n- Jack high is not strong for showdown value\n- This hand wins against random hands approximately 40-45% of the time\n\n**Opponent's Action Analysis:**\nMy opponent went all-in immediately pre-flop on hand 2. This is an extremely aggressive and unusual play that could mean:\n1. **Premium hand** (AA, KK, QQ, AK) - trying to maximize value\n2. **Bluff/Push strategy** - trying to bully with any two cards\n3. **Tilt** from losing hand 1\n\nGiven this is only hand 2 of 20, going all-in with 19,900 chips (nearly full stack) is a very high-variance play.\n\n**My Options:**\n1. **Fold (-1)**: Lose only the blind (100 chips), preserving 19,900 chips for future hands\n2. **All-in (-2)**: Risk all 19,900 chips with J6s\n\n**Risk/Reward Analysis:**\n- If I call and lose: I'm eliminated or severely crippled\n- If I fold: I lose ~100 chips but maintain strong position for 18 remaining hands\n- J6s is not strong enough to justify risking my entire tournament on hand 2\n- Even if opponent is bluffing 50% of the time, J6s doesn't perform well enough against their range to justify the risk\n\nReason:\nJ♠ 6♠ is too weak to call an all-in, especially this early in a 20-hand match. Even if my opponent is bluffing or playing aggressively, I need much stronger hands (at minimum pocket pairs 99+, AK, AQ suited) to risk my entire stack on hand 2. With 18 hands remaining, preserving chips and waiting for better spots is the optimal strategy. The risk-reward ratio heavily favors folding here.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
